  "chapter": {
    "number": 143,
    "content": [
      {
        "type": "hebrew_subtitle",
        "content": [
          "(144) A Psalm of David concerning Goliad."
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "verse",
        "number": 1,
        "content": [
          {
            "text": "Blessed be the Lord my God, who instructs my hands for battle, and my fingers for war.",
            "poem": 1
          },
          {
            "lineBreak": true
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "verse",
        "number": 2,
        "content": [
          {
            "text": "My mercy, and my refuge; my helper, and my deliverer; my protector, in whom I have trusted; who subdues my people under me.",
            "poem": 1
          },
          {
            "lineBreak": true
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "verse",
        "number": 3,
        "content": [
          {
            "text": "Lord, what is man, that you are made known to him? or the son of man, that you take account of him?",
            "poem": 1
          },
          {
            "lineBreak": true
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "verse",
        "number": 4,
        "content": [
          {
            "text": "Man is like to vanity: his days pass as a shadow.",
            "poem": 1
          },
          {
            "lineBreak": true
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "verse",
        "number": 5,
        "content": [
          {
            "text": "O Lord, bow your heavens, and come down: touch the mountains, and they shall smoke.",
            "poem": 1
          },
          {
            "lineBreak": true
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "verse",
        "number": 6,
        "content": [
          {
            "text": "Send lightning, and you shall scatter them: send forth your arrows, and you shall discomfit them.",
            "poem": 1
          },
          {
            "lineBreak": true
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "verse",
        "number": 7,
        "content": [
          {
            "text": "Send forth your hand from on high; rescue me, and deliver me out of great",
            "poem": 1
          },
          {
            "noteId": 257
          },
          {
            "text": "waters, out of the hand of strange children;",
            "poem": 1
          },
          {
            "lineBreak": true
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "verse",
        "number": 8,
        "content": [
          {
            "text": "whose mouth has spoken vanity, and their right hand is a right hand of iniquity.",
            "poem": 1
          },
          {
            "lineBreak": true
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "verse",
        "number": 9,
        "content": [
          {
            "text": "O God, I will sing a new song to you: I will play to you on a lute of ten strings.",
            "poem": 1
          },
          {
            "lineBreak": true
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "verse",
        "number": 10,
        "content": [
          {
            "text": "Even to him who gives",
            "poem": 1
          },
          {
            "noteId": 258
          },
          {
            "text": "salvation to kings: who redeems his servant David from the hurtful sword.",
            "poem": 1
          },
          {
            "lineBreak": true
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "verse",
        "number": 11,
        "content": [
          {
            "text": "Deliver me, and rescue me from the hand of strange children, whose mouth has spoken vanity, and their right hand is a right hand of iniquity;",
            "poem": 1
          },
          {
            "lineBreak": true
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "verse",
        "number": 12,
        "content": [
          {
            "text": "whose children are as plants, strengthened in their youth: their daughters are beautiful, sumptuously adorned after the similitude of a temple.",
            "poem": 1
          },
          {
            "lineBreak": true
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "verse",
        "number": 13,
        "content": [
          {
            "text": "Their garners are full, and bursting with one kind of store after another; their sheep are prolific, multiplying in their streets.",
            "poem": 1
          },
          {
            "lineBreak": true
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "verse",
        "number": 14,
        "content": [
          {
            "text": "Their oxen are fat: there is no falling down of a hedge, nor going out, nor cry in their",
            "poem": 1
          },
          {
            "noteId": 259
          },
          {
            "text": "folds.",
            "poem": 1
          },
          {
            "lineBreak": true
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "verse",
        "number": 15,
        "content": [
          {
            "text": "Men bless the people to whom this lot belongs, but blessed is the people whose God is the Lord.",
            "poem": 1
          }
        ]
      }
    ],
    "footnotes": [
      {
        "noteId": 257,
        "caller": "+",
        "text": "Gr. many.",
        "reference": {
          "chapter": 143,
          "verse": 7
        }
      },
      {
        "noteId": 258,
        "caller": "+",
        "text": "Or, victory.",
        "reference": {
          "chapter": 143,
          "verse": 10
        }
      },
      {
        "noteId": 259,
        "caller": "+",
        "text": "Or, habitations.",
        "reference": {
          "chapter": 143,
          "verse": 14
        }
      }
    ]
  },
